<hr>
<p>This provides a crucial insight:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Whenever we move to a new number, all candidates less than or equal to t=
his number should be removed from the candidates list.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Another key observation is that <strong>the list of candidates always ma=
intains a strictly decreasing order of values</strong>. This is because we =
always remove candidates less than or equal to each new value, ensuring val=
ues are added in decreasing order. We can see this more clearly in the fina=
l state of the candidates list of the previous example:</p>
<figure class=3D"py-1"><div style=3D"display: flex; justify-content: left;"=
><img alt=3D"Image represents a bar graph combined with a line graph illust=
